  <title>Modules menu</title>

  <p>A module is a Python file that adds a window of your own to PyGtk
  Posting, for a task the program does not cover. Each module found in the
  modules folder is listed in the <gui>Modules</gui> menu, under its file name
  with underscores shown as spaces: <file>stock_letters.py</file> appears as
  <gui>Stock letters</gui>.</p>

  <section id="folder">
  <title>The modules folder</title>
  <p>When PyGtk Posting is installed, the folder is
  <file>~/.config/posting/modules</file>; it is filled with the example
  module the first time PyGtk Posting starts. When it is run from its source
  code, the folder is <file>src/modules</file>. Modules are read when PyGtk
  Posting starts, so restart it after adding one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="writing">
  <title>Writing a module</title>
  <p>A module needs a class named <code>GUI</code>. Choosing the module in the
  menu creates one, passing the menu item that was chosen. The module reaches
  the books through the same database connection as the rest of the
  program:</p>
  <code mime="text/x-python">from gi.repository import Gtk
from db_connection import DB

class GUI:
	def __init__(self, menuitem):
		cursor = DB.cursor()
		cursor.execute("SELECT name FROM contacts")
		...</code>
  <p>The example module, <file>simple_example.py</file>, with its window in
  <file>simple_example.ui</file>, is a complete one to start from.</p>
  <note><p>A module runs with full access to the books. Only add modules you
  trust.</p></note>
  <p>A module useful to other businesses can be offered to the PyGtk Posting
  developers, to be reviewed and made part of the program.</p>
  </section>

